<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- What lets `UNNotificationContent.updating(from:)` render a banner as a
	     COMMUNICATION notification — the sender's face where the app icon
	     normally sits, rather than a thumbnail beside it.

	     The app's own App ID needs this capability and fails signing without
	     it. Whether THIS App ID
	     (com.walhallaa.chatTest-20241220.NotificationService) needs it too is
	     genuinely unsettled: Xcode's portal database lists the capability as
	     supported on an application and a watchkit2-extension and not on an
	     ordinary app extension — but it says the same of App Groups, which the
	     Share Extension next door has been signing with for as long as it has
	     existed. So the list is not to be trusted either way.

	     If the portal offers no Communication Notifications checkbox when you
	     create this App ID, DELETE this key rather than hunting for it. The
	     app's entitlement is what governs the rendering; this one is belt and
	     braces, and an entitlement no profile can carry is a build that cannot
	     sign. The console line in NotificationService.swift
	     ("communication notification refused") is what tells you if removing it
	     was the wrong call. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.usernotifications.communication</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
